    "evidence_text": "To better capture the complex representation of high-dimensional electroencephalography (EEG) data, we compute the joint variability of EEG electrodes into a channel cross-covariance matrix.\n\nAmong the various brain activity-monitoring modalities in BCI, electroencephalography (EEG)  ,  has demonstrated promising potential to differentiate between various brain activities through measurement of related electric fields.\n\nEEG is non-invasive, portable, low cost, and provides satisfactory temporal resolution.\n\nThis makes EEG suitable to realize BCI systems.\n\nEEG data, however, is\n\nJoint variability of electrodes\tMultichannel EEG data is high dimensional multivariate time series data whose dimensionality depends on the number of electrodes.\n\nBesides, we found that instead of using the raw multi-channel high-dimensional EEG requiring large training times and resource requirements, it is\n\nSince our EEG-phonological pairwise classification has an internal structure involving individual phonemes and words, it seems to be a reasonable choice of classifier.\n\nTraining and hyperparameter selection\tWe performed two sets of experiments with the single-trial EEG data.\n\nSince the model encounters the unseen data of a new subject for testing, and given the high inter-subject variability of the EEG data, a reduction in the accuracy was expected.",
